
---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Django Knit"
  description="An independent Django and TypeScript code generation project."
>
  <main class="site-width project-detail">
    <a class="project-back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a>

    <header class="project-detail-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / INDEPENDENT DEVELOPMENT</p>

      <h1>Django Knit<span class="project-slash">/</span></h1>

      <p class="project-lead">
        Model-driven code generation for Django and TypeScript,
        without forcing applications into a prescribed architecture.
      </p>

      <p class="project-status">Python · Django · DRF · TypeScript</p>
    </header>

    
    <section class="project-detail-section">
    <h2>Why I built it</h2>

    <div>
        <p>
        Django Knit started as an experiment inspired by Coalesce,
        a model-driven development framework. I appreciated the
        potential to eliminate repetitive API and frontend code,
        but frequently found its prescribed conventions limiting.
        </p>

        <p>
        One particularly frustrating experience involved something
        as ordinary as filtering records by a list of IDs. The
        generated API didn't support the operation I needed until
        the framework added support later. A tool intended to
        simplify development had become an obstacle to an otherwise
        straightforward requirement.
        </p>

        <p>
        Knit grew out of that experience: explore the benefits
        of model-driven generation while retaining the flexibility
        and familiar conventions of Django and Django REST Framework.
        </p>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>The approach</h2>
      <p>
        Django Knit uses explicit model registration and configuration
        to collect model metadata and drive code generation.
        The goal is predictable, readable output without requiring
        developers to surrender control of their applications.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
    <h2>From discovery to registration</h2>

    <div>
        <p>
        The first implementation automatically discovered Django
        models. That was convenient for proving the generator
        could work, but it also meant the default behavior
        included models the developer might never have intended
        to expose through an API.
        </p>

        <p>
        I eventually reversed that assumption. Model
        participation should be explicit, not automatic.
        A developer should decide which models belong in the
        generated API surface.
        </p>

        <p>
        Knit now provides decorator-based and settings-based
        registration through a shared registry. Settings-based
        registration also permits models from Django or
        third-party applications to participate without
        modifying their source code.
        </p>

        <p>
        This adds a configuration step, but makes the scope of
        generation intentional and easier to reason about.
        </p>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
        <h2>Generation pipeline</h2>

        <div>
            <p>
            Django Knit begins with explicitly registered Django
            models. Their configuration and model metadata are
            collected before being passed to the generation templates.
            </p>

            <figure class="knit-pipeline">
            <ol>
                <li>
                <span class="pipeline-number">01</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>Registration</strong>
                    <p>
                    Models opt in through the @knit_model decorator
                    or Django settings.
                    </p>
                </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                <span class="pipeline-number">02</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>Registry</strong>
                    <p>
                    Associates models with their developer-supplied
                    configuration.
                    </p>
                </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                <span class="pipeline-number">03</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>BuildHelper / ModelData</strong>
                    <p>
                    Inspects Django models and assembles metadata
                    for downstream generators.
                    </p>
                </div>
                </li>

                <li>
                <span class="pipeline-number">04</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>Code generation</strong>
                    <p>
                    Templates produce Django REST Framework and
                    TypeScript source files.
                    </p>
                </div>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <figcaption>
                Simplified build pipeline. The shared metadata
                contract is still under active development.
            </figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="project-detail-section">
        <h2>Developer configuration</h2>

        <div>
            <p>
            Registration is explicit. Models can be registered
            through a decorator or application settings, including
            models owned by third-party Django applications.
            </p>

            <pre class="project-code"><code>{`KNIT["MODELS_TO_REGISTER"] = {
            "auth.Group": {
                "searchable_fields": ("name",),
            },
            "contenttypes.ContentType": {},
        }`}</code></pre>

            <p>
            Settings-based registration allows developers to opt
            into generation without modifying the underlying model
            definitions.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>


    <section class="project-detail-section">
        <h2>API composition</h2>

        <div>
            <p>
            My preference is to keep resources independently
            accessible, return lightweight relationship references,
            and provide shallow nested representations when a
            particular view benefits from additional context.
            </p>

            <p>
            The goal isn't to minimize requests or response sizes
            at all costs. It's to balance database work, serialization,
            payload size, and network overhead without forcing every
            consumer to retrieve an entire object graph.
            </p>

            <p>
            This approach relies on flexible filtering and efficient
            collection retrieval. Where ordinary resource endpoints
            aren't sufficient, targeted actions or specialized
            endpoints can serve more demanding access patterns.
            </p>

            <p>
            Knit's simple and detailed generated types reflect this
            approach. Completing and validating the frontend integration
            remains part of the ongoing development work.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    
    <section class="project-detail-section">
        <h2>Configuration versus metadata</h2>

        <div>
            <p>
            As the generators grew more capable, configuration
            supplied by developers became increasingly mixed
            with information discovered from Django models.
            </p>

            <p>
            The current refactor separates these responsibilities.
            KnitModelConfig describes developer intent, while
            BuildHelper collects model and field information
            into structures such as ModelData and FieldData
            for downstream generation.
            </p>

            <p>
            The benefit is a clearer boundary between what a
            developer requests and what the generator discovers.
            It also gives the Python and TypeScript generators
            a common source of model information.
            </p>

            <p>
            This separation is still evolving. The metadata
            structures retain Django-specific objects, and
            some templates have yet to adopt the newer
            configuration model consistently.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-detail-section">
      <h2>Current state</h2>
      <p>
        Django Knit remains an active prototype. Explicit registration
        and model metadata collection are implemented, and existing
        generations demonstrate Python serializers, viewsets, TypeScript
        models, and API clients.
        <br>
        <br>
        Current work focuses on reconciling the generation templates
        with the newer registration and configuration architecture.
        A separate project's frontend API design provides the intended
        direction for completing the TypeScript integration, but that
        integration has not yet been completed and verified end to end.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
